{{also|émission|Emission}}

==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
First attested in 1607. From {{bor|en|frm|émission}}, from {{der|en|la|ēmissiō||sending forth}}, from {{m|la|ēmittō||send out}}, from {{m|la|ex||from, out of}} + {{m|la|mittō||send}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|en|/ɪˈmɪʃən/|/ɪˈmɪʃn̩/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-emission.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪʃən|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Something which is [[emit]]ted or sent out; [[issue]].
#: {{ux|en|the '''emission''' was mostly blood}}
# The [[act]] of emitting; the act of sending forth or putting into circulation.
#: {{ux|en|the '''emission''' of light from the sun}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''emission''' of heat from a fire}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=February|author=R. C. Riley|title=The London-Birmingham services - Past, Present and Future|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=99|text=[[Camden]] motive power depot has been much criticised for its '''emission''' of smoke in a residential neighbourhood and its complete dieselisation is rapidly taking place.}}

